"# Pattern A, Pattern B or island: which one to pick\n\nA Nodus component is a function that returns HTML. Does that HTML follow the application state on\nevery render? It depends on the pattern. This guide helps you choose and avoids the most common\ntrap: an interactive component that no longer updates.\n\n## 1. The two patterns in one sentence\n\n- **Pattern A — pure template.** Button, Badge, Card, Input, Table, Alert… Reef re-renders it on\n  every state change, like any piece of template. Nothing to do.\n- **Pattern B — template + behavior.** Dialog, Select, Tabs, DropdownMenu… The markup carries\n  `data-component` and `reef-ignore`; a JavaScript class handles keyboard, focus and opening.\n  `reef-ignore` tells Reef to **leave this node alone**: its content is frozen after the first\n  render.\n\nEach component page states its pattern; a Pattern B also lists the behavior to register\n(**Import** section).\n\n## 2. A Pattern B whose content depends on the state\n\nAs long as the content is fixed (navigation menu, static tabs), freezing it is not a problem. When\nit depends on the state, choose in this order:\n\n| Situation | Solution | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overlay: Dialog, Sheet, Drawer, AlertDialog, Sidebar | **Controlled mode**: `controlled: true`, `open` comes from the state (Sidebar: `collapsed`); the behavior emits `…:open-request` / `…:close-request` (Sidebar: `sidebar:toggle-request`) | `Dialog({ id: 'dlg', controlled: true, open: state.open, … })` |\n| Value component: Tabs, Accordion, Collapsible, ToggleGroup | **Value-controlled mode**: `controlled: true`, the state renders `selected` / `open` / `pressed`; the behavior emits `…:change-request` | `Tab({ value: 'profile', selected: state.tab === 'profile' })` |\n| Floating panel: DropdownMenu, Popover | **Controlled mode** (same principle, `open`) | |\n| Table sorting | **Event sorting**: `Table({ enableSort: 'event' })` emits `table:sort`, the application sorts | `TableHead({ sorted: 'asc' })` |\n| Content of an open Sheet or Dialog that changes, without a controlled mode | **Reactive island**: a second Reef `component()` mounted inside the shell | a Sheet's filters |\n| Select, Combobox, MultiSelect, DatePicker whose options change | **`registry.remount(id, html)`**: re-render the component with the new markup | cities depending on the country |\n\nIn a controlled mode, **the behavior no longer writes anything**: it asks, the application decides\nand re-renders. Without a listener for the request, nothing moves. That is intended: the state has\na single source.\n\n```js\ncomponent('#app', () => Dialog({ id: 'dlg', controlled: true, open: state.open,\n  title: 'Settings', children: `<p>Counter: ${state.counter}</p>` }));\n\ndocument.addEventListener('dialog:open-request',  () => { state.open = true; });\ndocument.addEventListener('dialog:close-request', () => { state.open = false; });\n```\n\nA stable `id` is required in controlled mode: internal ids derive from it.\n\n## 3. Lists and editors: no component, a recipe\n\nNodus has no \"editable list\" or \"editor\" component: a Pattern B would freeze the rows outside the\nstate. In Reef, a list is `state.items.map(row)`, with a stable `id` per row and fields in\n`sync: true`. Complete, tested recipes: `demos/11-recettes/` (repeatable list, key / value, tree),\nand the [lists and nested structures](../../../GUIDELINES.md#listes-et-structures-imbriquées-depuis-310)\nsection.\n\n## 4. In short\n\n1. Is the component a Pattern A? **Nothing to do.**\n2. A Pattern B whose content does not change? **Nothing to do** (remember to register its\n   behavior).\n3. Its content changes? **Controlled mode** if it exists, otherwise a **reactive island** (content\n   of an overlay) or **`registry.remount()`** (options of a list).\n4. An editable collection? **No component**: the Reef recipe.\n\nGoing further: [frozen or controlled components](../../../GUIDELINES.md#composants-figés-ou-contrôlés),\n[ADR-001](../../../ADR/001-contenu-reactif-pattern-b.md) and\n[ADR-006](../../../ADR/006-mode-controle-a-valeur.md).\n"